# EXAMPLE ONLY -- this file is NOT shipped in the image and is NOT read by
# anything. It is the shape of the file plus the reasoning behind it.
#
# Every address, MAC and router-id below has been replaced by a placeholder.
# Put your own in
#
#     /mnt/data/config/network.conf
#
# which lives on the data partition, so it survives an upgrade, a rollback and
# a reinstall -- and is never overwritten by an image built for this model.
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE USED TO CARRY ONE PHYSICAL SWITCH'S IDENTITY, IN A PUBLIC REPO.
# An image built from it made a second switch a duplicate of the first: same
# management address, same MAC, same OSPF router-id in the same area. Keep real
# values in <name>.site.conf, which .gitignore excludes, or on the switch.
#
# Network configuration for this board, captured from the EdgeNOS installation
# it replaces so the two can be compared directly.
#
# Read off the running switch on 2026-09-02, from `ip addr`, `ip link` and
# EdgeNOS's own /etc/edged/swp-addrs.conf. This is the record of what this
# router does today: bringing NOSaic up here means reproducing it.
#
#   iface <name> <address/prefix> [mtu <n>] [mac <addr>]
#   route <prefix|default> via <gateway> [dev <iface>]

# ── Management ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The P2020's own eTSEC, driven by gianfar, behind a BCM54610C PHY.
#
# **eth0 here, end0 on the EdgeNOS box.** Same hardware, same driver, different
# name: EdgeNOS runs systemd, whose predictable naming renames a device-tree
# NIC to end0, and NOSaic runs s6 with nothing that renames anything. The
# kernel calls it eth0 and it stays eth0. Copying the name out of `ip addr` on
# the running switch is therefore wrong, and wrong in the quietest possible
# way: the interface simply never gets configured and the service reports it
# absent alongside the front-panel ports that genuinely are.
#
# Static, and stated here, because a switch that renumbers itself on a lease
# change is not a router you can find again. The MAC is the board's own, out of
# board_eeprom.
#
# MOVED on 2026-09-11, from <address>/<prefix> via <address>. That address came from
# the EdgeNOS capture this file was built from and had stopped being real: the
# port had link at 1000 and rx_packets=0, with the ARP entry for its own
# gateway never resolving, so the box had been reachable only over the serial
# console. It was not noticed because the front panel was up the whole time and
# the switch was doing its job -- OSPF, forwarding, adjacencies -- on ports that
# had nothing to do with management. A dead management port on a working router
# is quiet.
iface eth0 <address>/<prefix> mac <this switch's MAC>
route default via <address> dev eth0

# Management stays on the management port.
#
# Once this box runs OSPF it learns a route to the build host's subnet through
# its own front panel, and the kernel prefers it: <address> goes "via
# <address> dev swp1", so every ssh and every file copy is carried by the
# CPU punt path instead of the management NIC. It works, which is what makes it
# dangerous -- latency went from 0.3 ms to 7.7 ms here and a 28 MB transfer
# stalled at 49 KB, which reads as a flaky network rather than a routing
# decision the switch made about itself.
#
# A static route wins on metric over the OSPF one, so this pins it back
# whatever the topology advertises.
route <address>/<prefix> via <address> dev eth0

# ── Loopback ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Carries the OSPF router-id, which is why it is a loopback: the id must not
# move when a front-panel port goes down.
iface lo <address>/<prefix>

# ── Front panel ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MTU 1600 throughout, matching the OSPF neighbours. EdgeNOS leaves the
# unaddressed ports at 9000; only the routed ones are set to 1600, and a
# mismatch here is an adjacency that reaches ExStart and stops.
#
# These interfaces do not exist until the datapath daemon creates them, and
# there is no nosd-tdp yet -- so on this board today none of them appear. That
# is why net_wait_secs is short in board.yml: the wait is correct behaviour
# and, until the datapath lands, it is a wait for something that cannot happen.
#
# swp49 is the uplink to the Cisco Nexus and carries the default route.
iface swp49 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600
iface swp51 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600
iface swp52 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600

# 10G links to the rest of the lab.
iface swp1 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600
iface swp2 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600
iface swp4 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600
iface swp5 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600
iface swp6 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600
iface swp7 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600
# swp8 faces the Arista 7050SX2's et1, which is that board's way back to the
# build host when its management port is down.
iface swp8 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600

# ── IPv6 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This switch also carries IPv6 on lo and swp1/2/4/6/7/8/49, in a globally
# routable /48. Those addresses are deliberately NOT in this file: they are the
# site's real assignment rather than board data, and this repository is public.
#
# They live beside this file in network.site.conf, which is gitignored, and the
# switch reads /mnt/data/config/network.conf in preference to this one -- so
# the complete configuration goes on the box without passing through here.
